We also need to handle null case: if old element null, after remains null; else equals old element. That's essentially equality of objects (including null). In Java, we can use .equals but null cannot call equals. So we can assert ( \old(this.get(i)) == null ? this.get(i) == null : this.get(i).equals(\old(this.get(i))) ). But spec language maybe using == for reference equality? In examples they used .equals for objects. For null case they used .equals? In example they used this.query(i).equals(\old(this.query(i))) which assumes non-null? But they didn't handle null. However spec may allow .equals on possibly null? In JML, you can use \old(this.get(i)) == null ? this.get(i) == null : this.get(i).equals(\old(this.get(i))). Let's follow that pattern.

